A||||7||Jaei Sun||What could this be??  Shifting problem??||||5||
Z||000000||Jaei Sun||03-20-2001||06:26 PM||||My 87 2.8 5-speed seems to be having a few problems as of late.<P>When the car is cold it runs like a dream.  Smooth Shifts (moving and from a stop), smooth coast at low speeds.<P>Problems occur once the car has warmed up.<P>(I Used the search function and couldn't find any matches -- at least with my keywords)<P>Once warmed up and moving the shifts are extremely smooth (Thanks to Redline MTL) but once I come to a stop, shifting into first (or any gear for that matter) takes a lot of force.  Clutch pedal all the way down, lubed linkage (at tranny).  I can't figure it out.  But once I do manage to get it into first to take off shifting resumes being smooth again.<P>That's the one problem... this might be releated to this one:<P>Speeds below 20 mph in second or first cause the car to chug, So I have to either ride the clutch a little, brake, or give it enough gas to stop the chuggin.<P>Anyone have any Ideas what these could be??  I just got my Fiero 3 months ago so I don't know very much about it.  And I don't want to be pissed off at my car for these little problems!<P>Any help would be great!~!<p>[This message has been edited by Jaei Sun (edited 03-20-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||gixxer||03-20-2001||06:56 PM||||Clutch isn't disengaging all the way. <BR>The disc warms up with all the slipping, and gets a tad thicker. <BR>So you have to shift gears, for the most part, without a clutch (grunt!--K-Bang!).<BR>Might try bleeding it. <BR>Or the diaphram fingers on the pressure plate are going south.<BR>That's my guess.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||03-20-2001||08:27 PM||||You may already know this . . .<P>The original clutch pedal arm was made of aluminum; it bends in service.  A stronger steel replacement is available.  When you install a steel pedal, you may also need to install a bushing where the master cylinder pushrod connects to the pedal.  Rodney Dickman sells bushings.<P>At a recent Georgia Fiero Club meeting, our local ace Fiero mechanic replaced the club president's pedal in the American Pie parking lot; it took about 45 minutes.  According to the Prez, it made a huge difference.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||BBBAD GT||03-20-2001||10:30 PM||||Jason:  I had a shifting problem about 5 months into owning the car.  I couldn't figure it out either for a while, except when I checked the clutch fluid.  It was really low, so I cleaned it up and added new fluid and all was well.  Also, that cable linkage isn't the best in the world.  I could never get it into reverse on the first try, and always had to shift it into first gear first before rowing it back into reverse.  When that still didn't get it into reverse, I'd just lift the clutch a time or two in nutural, then it would go into R.  Hope this helps.  <BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">      <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT (sold)</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wolfhound||03-21-2001||05:41 AM||||I had a similar problem when I first got the 87 GT. In heavy stop and go traffic I was loosing my clutch. The slave cylinder was overheating and posssibly had some water in the system. I flushed the system and put a heat shield on the slave by using the mat from an EGR tube that had broken. That cured it.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||03-22-2001||12:35 AM||||hmm.. my GT will pull just fine from 10mph in second.  I still think your problem is mostly worn synchros.  My GT doesn't like to shift into 1st, and the 3rd gear synchro's are gone..  As for reverse, it has no synchro's anyways, which is why you can't always get into it the first time, and why usually grinds when you shift into it..||||reg||1
Z||000006||rodmcneill||03-22-2001||12:44 AM||||An 87 GT should already have the steel clutch pedal in it. I think they changed from aluminum in 85 early 86?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Jaei Sun||03-22-2001||01:17 AM||||Thanks all for the suggestions!  I'm going to try with the cheapest method first, bleeding the system.  My Dad suggested that I get some heat wrap and put a layer on the slave cyl.  He told me that since it's like 1 1/2" away from the exhaust piping it could be the heat that is causing it.  Perhaps by boiling the fluid (on really hot days/long drives) and causing air bubbles to form in the line.<P>But the second would seem to me like a less likely situation cuz don't you think GM would have made sure that the heat from the exhaust piping didn't interfere with the slave cyl fuctions??<p>[This message has been edited by Jaei Sun (edited 03-22-2001).]||||reg||5
